<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pietro Verri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1728">1728</a>–<a href="/years/1797">1797</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pietro Verri was a Milanese economist, historian and writer and a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-enlightenment">Italian Enlightenment</a>. With his brother Alessandro he formed the Accademia dei Pugni and its journal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/il-caffe">Il Caffè</a> (1764–1766), and he encouraged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-beccaria">Cesare Beccaria</a>'s work on penal reform. He served in the Austrian administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> and wrote Meditations on Political Economy (1771) and a history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pietro-verri.jpg" alt="Pietro Verri" width="320" height="577" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nansfeld · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pietro_Verri.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Count Pietro Verri was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> on 12 December 1728, the eldest son of Gabriele Verri and Barbara Dati Della Somaglia, into a conservative noble family. He studied at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> college in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monza">Monza</a>, at the college of the Barnabites in Milan from 1740 to 1744 and at the college of Nazareno in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and began to rebel against his strict religious upbringing in his twenties. To escape the legal studies his father had chosen for him, he volunteered for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> but left after a year.</p><p>In September 1759 he met the economist Henry Lloyd, who became a lifelong friend, and came to think political economy the centre of all serious social questions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-hume">David Hume</a> and Helvétius shaped his views. His satirical almanacs scandalised Milanese society, and in 1761 he and his brother Alessandro founded the Società dei Pugni, whose journal carried writing by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-beccaria">Cesare Beccaria</a>, Alfonso Longo and Pietro Secchi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Entering the public administration in 1764, Verri proposed ending the collection of taxes through intermediaries. His Elementi del Commercio appeared in 1769 and the Meditazioni sull'economia politica went through five editions in a year. He held that price rises with need, meaning effective demand, and falls with plenty, that demand governs supply, and that population is the best measure of a nation's success. Joseph Schumpeter counted him among the first economists to work out a balance of payments. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a> owned two editions of the book.</p><p>In the last issues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/il-caffe">Il Caffè</a> in 1766 he argued for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/variolation">smallpox inoculation</a>, citing 19 deaths in 24,167 inoculations. His Osservazioni sulla tortura of 1777 stressed the uselessness and cruelty of torture, and his ideas on pleasure and pain were later taken up by Kant, Schopenhauer and Wundt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The ecclesiastical reforms of Joseph II prompted his Dialogue between Pius VI and Joseph II of 1782, but the emperor's growing despotism led him to leave the Austrian service in 1786. After the French invasion he returned as a member of the Milanese municipality and helped to found the Cisalpine Republic, welcoming the chance of reform while disapproving of Jacobin excesses. The Storia di Milano was published in two volumes, in 1783 and 1798. He died of a stroke at a meeting of the municipality on the night of 28 June 1797 and was buried at Ornago beside his first wife.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pietro Verri” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the eighteenth century Austrian Lombardy became a centre of the Italian Enlightenment: <b>Pietro Verri</b>’s journal Il Caffè and Cesare Beccaria’s On Crimes and Punishments (1764) influenced penal reform across Europe, and the Teatro alla Scala opened in 1778.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.47,9.19&amp;year=1772" aria-label="Milan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4768 560 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pietro Verri"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5218 830h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5218 830h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.47,9.19&amp;year=1772">What was here in 1772? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
